  "days_covered": 3,
  "primer": {
    "text": "The standoff centers on the United States and Israel, Iran, and Iran-aligned Houthis in Yemen over two oil chokepoints. Washington and its ally aim to degrade Iran's nuclear and military capacity and keep shipping moving; Tehran uses control of the Strait of Hormuz to deter attacks and extract concessions; the Houthis press the Red Sea front. It sharpened after a U.S.-Israeli operation in late February 2026 struck Iranian nuclear, military and leadership sites, after which Iran restricted Hormuz. The strait carries 20 million barrels a day — a fifth of world oil and LNG — far more than pipeline bypasses can replace, so disruption lifts energy and freight costs. Saudi Arabia's Red Sea pipeline workaround is vulnerable to Houthi attacks.",
    "built_at": "2026-10-04T07:26:06.472952+00:00"
  },
  "current": {
    "date": "2026-10-03",
    "bottom_line": "Hormuz remains effectively closed with no formal US counter-offer conveyed via mediators as of Oct 2-3, keeping the core oil and LNG disruption in place. The confrontation widened off the water: the UK investigation into an alleged plot at RAF Fairford advanced with a new arrest and a public attribution to Iran that Tehran denies, while the Red Sea front stayed under Houthi pressure with the Saudi bypass pipeline still degraded. Confidence is high on the shipping and market data and on the investigative steps; lower on attribution and on private US intentions.",
